title: 'PASS: Claude Code works inside the hardened macOS session sandbox and the denies hold'
summary: |-
  p10m-claude-compat passes on macOS 15.7.3 with claude 2.1.302. Three paid turns, about $0.63 on the founder's subscription.

  Under the hardened session profile (§15.5 plus the sysmond mach-lookup deny plus the tmux unix-socket deny), `claude -p` authenticated and answered (exit 0, 2 turns). Its Bash tool ran node v26, git and python, wrote in the workdir, and spawned and killed a child. Meanwhile:
  - the stand-in endpoint port was unreachable (curl 000/exit 7, 0 server hits);
  - pgrep saw 0 processes, against 567 in the control;
  - the denied directory was unwritable;
  - ps fails, as expected for setuid binaries.

  The control run (unsandboxed) reached the port (HTTP 200) and saw every process. One probe bug was found and fixed: SBPL path rules match resolved paths, so temp dirs must be realpath'd (/var -> /private/var). That trap applies to the kernel's real profile generator too.

  Conclusion: the M1.7 session sandbox approach is viable on macOS once the p10m fixes are adopted in the spec.
symbols_touched:
  - '#gnomon-probes'
  - '#paradigm-harness'
files_created:
  - packages/gnomon/probes/p10m-claude-compat/probe.mjs
learnings:
  - 'SBPL subpath rules match resolved paths: always realpath a directory (e.g. /var/folders -> /private/var/folders) before writing it into a sandbox profile, or the deny silently doesn''t apply.'
